{
 "format": "bmc-sensor-audit/walk-series/1",
 "_provenance": "28 consecutive Redfish walks of the same emulated machine, captured 2026-08-18 from upstream bmcweb in openbmc CI image obmc-phosphor-image-bletchley-20260818025123.static.mtd, running under QEMU 10.2.1 as machine bletchley-bmc, after the FRU write that instantiates the baseboard. Every reading below is what that firmware served. GROUND TRUTH, CONTROLLED: for the first 12 walks the sensor named in driven_sensor was set to a new value through the QEMU monitor before each walk (qom-set on /machine/unattached/device[18] temperature0, millidegrees, the sequence in driven_millidegrees). From walk 13 it was not touched again. Nothing else was driven in either phase. The jitter is deliberate and not a ramp: a monotonic climb toward the declared 54 C upper critical makes the engine report a trend instead, which was observed before this sequence was chosen, and a strict alternation would trip the period-2 oscillation term. WHAT THIS IS NOT: a sensor failure. Freezing a register through an emulator monitor is an experiment. It also proves nothing about physical sensor behaviour, population density on real boards, or any Redfish dialect other than bmcweb. SHAPE: the walks differ from one another in exactly two fields, sensors.reading and sensors.health, re-verified at generation time; everything else is identical across all 28, so the invariant part is stored once as walk_skeleton and the varying part as per-sensor series. Storing 28 whole walks would have cost 304,957 bytes against this file's 34,650, a 61 percent increase on the 499,588 bytes of every tracked file in the repository at the time, to carry 27 more copies of a tree shape walk_qemu_bletchley.json already proves. All three figures are byte counts, not disk usage.",
